{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 99,
    "dDurationMs": 1861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What is inside a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1960,
    "dDurationMs": 1309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inevitable crushing doom?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3269,
    "dDurationMs": 2021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gateways to other universes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5290,
    "dDurationMs": 1719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weird, multidimensional libraries?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7009,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’ve ever wanted to know then you might\nbe in luck - Some physicists have argued that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11840,
    "dDurationMs": 2533,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re inside a black hole right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19220,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s ridiculously successful general\ntheory of relativity has never failed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25150,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we know it must: at the centers of black\nholes and at the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28930,
    "dDurationMs": 5469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both of these involve matter being packed\nto infinite densities - they are singularities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34399,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the mathematics of GR breaks down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36969,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out that black holes and the\nBig Bang have more in common than vexing Einstein."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41949,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have mathematical similarities to ech other that have led some physicists to believe that the big"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47809,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bang is in fact the singularity of an absurdly\ngigantic black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52089,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s figure out how crazy this proposition\nreally is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56510,
    "dDurationMs": 1789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58299,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Collapse any chunk of matter far enough and\nit gets stuck in its own gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61859,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general relativity, that looks like a point\nof infinite density surrounded by an event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66229,
    "dDurationMs": 1371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67600,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of the event horizon as the surface\nwhere the flow of space itself is like a river"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71810,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73960,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nothing can swim against that flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75820,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the outside, the black hole looks like\nan orb of blackness at a particular location"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80670,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in space, due to the fact that nothing that\nnot even light can travel back out from below"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85450,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86680,
    "dDurationMs": 4229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe also has a singularity and an\nevent horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90909,
    "dDurationMs": 5161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The singularity is the Big Bang, which we\nthink of as a point in time at the beginning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96070,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe when all matter was compressed\nto infinite density and all points in space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101469,
    "dDurationMs": 1851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overlapped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103320,
    "dDurationMs": 3659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the Big Bang space expanded, and it’s\nstill expanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106979,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That expansion gives us our event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109859,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If space is expanding evenly everywhere, then\nthere are distant regions of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114079,
    "dDurationMs": 3941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are being propelled away from us faster\nthan the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118020,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means there’s a surface at a particular\ndistance that represents the limit of our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122619,
    "dDurationMs": 6711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observations - in that we can never know about\nany event that happens beyond that surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129330,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the cosmological event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132510,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it’s somewhat more complicated - for\nexample, we’re still receiving light from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136820,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects that are now beyond that horizon,\nbecause it was emitted by those objects before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141130,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they crossed the horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142660,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, the accelerating expansion of the universe\nmeans the cosmological event horizon is closer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to us than the spot where recession equals\nthe speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153920,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the effect is the same: no events that\nhappen now beyond that horizon can ever be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160260,
    "dDurationMs": 1010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161270,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Complications aside, there are striking similarities\nbetween the black hole and the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166160,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The obvious difference is that the black hole\nsingularity seems to us to be a point of infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171210,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density in space, while the big bang singularity is a time of infinite density that included"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176680,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178440,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, that difference isn’t as different\nas it sounds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181780,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both the big bang and black hole singularities do occupy all of space - the difference is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188390,
    "dDurationMs": 5541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang singularity exists in the past\nfor all of space, while the black hole singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193931,
    "dDurationMs": 4009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists in the future for all of the balck hole space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197940,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, now that last part probably needs a little more explanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202000,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about an idea called geodesic\nincompleteness in a couple of episodes - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207210,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our recent one on the center of the universe,\nand also when we asked what happened before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212200,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213200,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to need that idea again, so\nhere’s a refresher."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217210,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general relativity, objects that are not\nbeing acted on by a force follow something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222060,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called a geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224250,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the straightest paths that can be\ntaken through a curved spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228440,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a sense geodesics form the grid that defines\nthe fabric of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234310,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s possible to define a geodesic at some\npoint in space and time - say, the arc of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240230,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a ball thrown through the air - and then you\ncan trace that geodesic forwards and backwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That trace will define the path of the thrown\nball, but you can also extrapolate beyond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252280,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ball’s trajectory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253820,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would be the path that the ball would\nfollow if it could pass through the planet,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258500,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the path through the ground that could\nhave brought the ball to its current position."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264250,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you can keep tracing the geodesic into\nthe infinite future or all the way back to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269080,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang - it’s defined for all past\nand future times independently of the ball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276160,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesics generally don’t just end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279390,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except at singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281960,
    "dDurationMs": 7670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, in general relativity, singularities are defined as the end points of geodesics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289630,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All geodesics in the universe come together\nand terminate at the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293250,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call the Big Bang a past, space-like singularity\nbecause it occupies all of space -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300270,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301940,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A black hole contains a future, space-like\nsingularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305970,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means that all geodesics within a black\nhole spacetime end at the singularity in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311840,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312840,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, that doesn’t mean you’re doomed to\nbe crushed by a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316230,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, you might, but you might not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319420,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole singularity is the all-encompassing\nfuture for the spacetime that lives beneath"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325060,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon in the same way that the\nbig bang is the encompassing past for the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329970,
    "dDurationMs": 1457,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the outside universe. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331427,
    "dDurationMs": 4326,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the black hole and the big bang singularities are starting to look more alike. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335753,
    "dDurationMs": 4247,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the difference being, their residence in the future versus the past. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340000,
    "dDurationMs": 3424,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the fact that black holes are embedded within a greater universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343424,
    "dDurationMs": 2913,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the big bang is the entire universe. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346337,
    "dDurationMs": 4525,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we make a black hole look more like a universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350862,
    "dDurationMs": 6208,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll need to make the black hole interior\nmathematically indistinguishable from a universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357070,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for somebody inside that black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359680,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first step is to send that singularity\nto the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363300,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A time-reversed black hole is easy enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a white hole, and it’s a valid a\nsolution to the Einstein equations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370880,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Naturally we’ve discussed them before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373030,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The past, space-like singularity of the white\nhole is surrounded by an event horizon that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378700,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the opposite to a black hole event horizon\n- it can only be crossed from the inside to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383560,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the outside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384980,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space flows at the speed of light across the\nevent horizon from within."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388750,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s starting to look like our universe\n- a past, space-like singularity and an event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393570,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon that can’t be crossed from the outside."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395950,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s still an “outside” in which\nthe white hole appears as a bright, localized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402880,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404020,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The outside that doesn’t share the same singularity origin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408220,
    "dDurationMs": 6672,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, anyone inside the white hole wouldn't know that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414892,
    "dDurationMs": 4568,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So could they be fooled into thinking they are in a regular universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419460,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first glance, despite the similarities\nthe interior of the white hole looks nothing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423570,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426490,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the white hole was “discovered” by messing around with the coordinates of the OG black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431990,
    "dDurationMs": 3459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole solution by Karl Schwarzschild."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435449,
    "dDurationMs": 5601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same trick gave us wormholes and mirror universes,\nby the way, and we mapped these weird spaces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441050,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442860,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of this type of white hole looks\nnothing like our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447240,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing its made of pure spacetime - no\nmatter included , and it;s highly inhomogeneous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454040,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The curvature changes dramatically as you\napproach the singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457530,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our universe appears to be highly homogeneous\n- matter and energy are very evenly spread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463260,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out, and it was even more evenly spread out\nin the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468090,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the space-time curvature is nearly flat,\nso no crazy tidal forces like in a white or black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474940,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spacetime of our universe is well described\nby the Friedmann-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480740,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric, which we also talked about in the\ncenter of the universe episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485020,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is actually a way to fit an FLRW\nmetric inside a black or white hole so that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492580,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those inside it couldn’t tell the difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495790,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The very first “realistic” mathematical\ndescription of a black hole formation was"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500580,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovered in 1939 by Robert Oppenheimer - of\natomic bomb fame - along with his student"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506520,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hartland Snyder They approximated the collapse\nof a star by modeling it as a spherical cloud"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512329,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of matter with a perfectly homogeneous density\nand zero pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516969,
    "dDurationMs": 5211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The thing collapses under its own gravity\nand an event horizon forms around it, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522180,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within the collapsing cloud the matter remains\nhomogeneous and the spacetime is flat until"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528480,
    "dDurationMs": 2649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it becomes a singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531129,
    "dDurationMs": 2221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now real stars don’t look like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533350,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They get denser towards the center and have\nplenty of pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536290,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Oppenheimer-Snyder solution gave our\nfirst insights into black hole formation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541829,
    "dDurationMs": 4771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it also showed another weird other property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546600,
    "dDurationMs": 6229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This assumption of homogeneity and zero pressure\nwas the same that Alexander Friedmann made"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552829,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when he first solved the Einstein equations\nfor the whole universe, and it’s the assumption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557920,
    "dDurationMs": 2829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind the FLRW metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560749,
    "dDurationMs": 6171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact you can describe the spacetime of\na collapsing star by patching an FLRW metlric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566920,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside a Schwarzschild metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570550,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s true even after the black hole’s\nevent horizon forms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can have what looks like a black hole from the inside, but looks like comfortably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579720,
    "dDurationMs": 3429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flat space inside the still-collapsing star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583149,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it works for the black hole then it should\nwork for the white hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587449,
    "dDurationMs": 4851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just flip the timel axis and you have a white\nhole containing a bubble of expanding space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592300,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that looks much like our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595180,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If such a white hole was big enough, it could\nlook exactly like our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600560,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was the basic proposal by Indian physicist\nRaj Pathria in his Black Hole Cosmology hypothesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607240,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back in 1972."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609500,
    "dDurationMs": 4459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as I’ve been able to tell, no one\nhas convincingly shown that this can’t be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613959,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614959,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also the idea that universes are\nborn as white holes produced after the collapse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619850,
    "dDurationMs": 1359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621209,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the Cosmological Natural Selection\nidea by Lee Smolin, and yeah, we did an episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627759,
    "dDurationMs": 5541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case, black holes don’t form singularities,\nbut rather bounce back outward to create"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633300,
    "dDurationMs": 6019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new spacetimes from the resulting white\nhole - which itself creates new black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639319,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640319,
    "dDurationMs": 6151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we need one last ingredient to be able\nto answer “maybe” to the question we started"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646470,
    "dDurationMs": 2279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with: Are we in a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648749,
    "dDurationMs": 5981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1999, Stephen Hawking once showed that\nif a black hole is leaking its mass via Hawking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654730,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation in perfect equilibrium with the\nradiation that it absorbs - for example, from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660410,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cosmic microwave background - then the\nline between the white hole and black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664889,
    "dDurationMs": 1521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes unclear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666410,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They could be the same object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668810,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you lump all of this together - a very\nspecific construction for white holes to make"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673899,
    "dDurationMs": 6331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them look like our universe from within, and Hawking’s argument that equates white holes with black holes,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680230,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then there’s a roundabout way to argue we\nmight not NOT be in a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685790,
    "dDurationMs": 2109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is the universe a black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687899,
    "dDurationMs": 1850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or a white hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689749,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690749,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not absolutely not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692429,
    "dDurationMs": 6641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no good reason to believe that this\nis the case, so we shouldn’t believe the universe is a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699070,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d need some deeper explanatory value\nto this hypothesis to really take it seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705860,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cosmological natural selection might be a\npath to that, but we also need some evidence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710440,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for that in order to proceed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712680,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However if we are in a black hole then there’s\na huge upside: we now know what the interior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718470,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a black holes look like. It looks like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722300,
    "dDurationMs": 3149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are indeed libraries in this black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725449,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps, one day, one of the books in those\nlibraries will also answer the next question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731819,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we’re within the event horizon of a black/white hole, what’s on the outside?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736989,
    "dDurationMs": 7321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe a universe inhabited by people asking exactly the same question, with the same answer, that they are in a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744310,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on ad infinitum in a series of black\nholes, forming an infinitely nested space time"
    } ]
  } ]
}
